ipa: simple: Set the analogue gain code nearest to the requested gain

The camera sensor helper's gainCode() truncates, and the IPA reads the
gain of the code it set back for the next frame. On a sensor with a
reciprocal gain curve, such as the IMX681 (1024 / (1024 - code)), one
code is worth more than the AGC's smallest step (a hundredth of the gain
range, 0.15 on the IMX681) above about 12.4x: a small increase truncates
to the same code, the AGC reads the same gain back and asks for the same
small step again. The analogue gain then stays below its maximum, and
the digital gain, which the AGC adds only once the analogue gain is at
its maximum, never starts. In a model of the AGC, dim scenes of about 12
to 17 lux settled between 12.3x and 15.75x without digital gain, about
0.4 stop darker than the target asks for.

Set the code whose gain is nearest to the requested one instead. Half a
code step is at most 0.125 up to 16x, less than the smallest step, so
every step moves the gain, and 15.9x reaches 16x.

[sp11: for the Surface Pro 11's front camera (IMX681); the rear camera's
OV13858 gain (code / 128) has steps far below the AGC's]
---
 src/ipa/simple/soft_simple.cpp | 27 +++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/ipa/simple/soft_simple.cpp b/src/ipa/simple/soft_simple.cpp
index 629e1a32d..ed44c35ed 100644
--- a/src/ipa/simple/soft_simple.cpp
+++ b/src/ipa/simple/soft_simple.cpp
@@ -73,6 +73,7 @@ protected:
 
 private:
 	void updateExposure(double exposureMSV);
+	int32_t gainCode(double gain) const;
 
 	DebayerParams *params_;
 	SwIspStats *stats_;
@@ -322,12 +323,34 @@ void IPASoftSimple::processStats(const uint32_t frame,
 
 	auto &againNew = frameContext.sensor.gain;
 	ctrls.set(V4L2_CID_EXPOSURE, frameContext.sensor.exposure);
-	ctrls.set(V4L2_CID_ANALOGUE_GAIN,
-		  static_cast<int32_t>(camHelper_ ? camHelper_->gainCode(againNew) : againNew));
+	ctrls.set(V4L2_CID_ANALOGUE_GAIN, gainCode(againNew));
 
 	setSensorControls.emit(ctrls);
 }
 
+/*
+ * The sensor's gain code for a gain: the code whose gain is nearest. The
+ * helper's gainCode() truncates, and the gain of the code set is what the
+ * AGC reads back for the next frame. Where one code is worth more than the
+ * AGC's smallest step, as near the top of a reciprocal gain curve, a
+ * truncated small increase would give the same code again and again.
+ */
+int32_t IPASoftSimple::gainCode(double gain) const
+{
+	if (!camHelper_)
+		return static_cast<int32_t>(gain);
+
+	const int32_t max =
+		sensorInfoMap_.find(V4L2_CID_ANALOGUE_GAIN)->second.max().get<int32_t>();
+	int32_t code = static_cast<int32_t>(camHelper_->gainCode(gain));
+	if (code >= max)
+		return max;
+	if (camHelper_->gain(code + 1) - gain < gain - camHelper_->gain(code))
+		code++;
+
+	return code;
+}
+
 std::string IPASoftSimple::logPrefix() const
 {
 	return "IPASoft";
